---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '@esa/docs/base';
import Section from '@esa/docs/Section.astro';
import { componentApi } from '../../data/component-api';
import { isCustomElement } from '../../data/angular-snippet';

// Counted, not asserted. The split between "real custom element" and
// "compile-time only" is the single fact this page exists to convey, and a
// hand-typed number here would be wrong within a month.
const slugs = Object.keys(componentApi);
const elementCount = slugs.filter(isCustomElement).length;
const astroCount = slugs.length - elementCount;

// The names a reader is most likely to reach for and find in the wrong bucket.
const NOTABLE_ASTRO = ['esa-button', 'esa-badge', 'esa-icon', 'esa-form-field', 'esa-card']
  .filter((s) => componentApi[s] && !isCustomElement(s));
---
<DocsLayout title="Using Ecology from Angular — Ecology" activeHref="/guide/angular">
  <nav class="crumbs"><a href={withBase('/')}>Ecology</a> <span>/</span> <span>Guide</span> <span>/</span> <span>Angular</span></nav>
  <h1>Using Ecology from Angular</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Most ESA apps are Angular. The interactive half of this kit is standards-based Web
    Components, which Angular can consume directly — so component pages show the Angular
    binding beside the HTML one. This page is the part that does not fit on a component
    page: which components you can actually use, and what does not work yet.
  </p>

  <Section id="two-halves" title="Only half the kit is an element you can use">
    <p>
      This is the first thing to get straight, because the names run against intuition.
      Of the {slugs.length} components, <strong>{elementCount}</strong> are Lit web
      components that register a real custom element, and <strong>{astroCount}</strong>
      are <code>.astro</code> — compiled to HTML at build time, with
      <em>no runtime element behind them at all</em>. Writing
      <code>&lt;esa-badge&gt;</code> in an Angular template does not render a badge. It
      renders nothing, and reports no error.
    </p>
    <p>
      The presentational ones are the ones you would reach for first:
      {NOTABLE_ASTRO.map((s, i) => (
        <>{i > 0 && ', '}<code>{s}</code></>
      ))} are all in that bucket.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the Angular tab appears only on pages for components that really are elements.
      Its absence is information: that component is a markup-and-tokens contract to
      reproduce, not a package to import. The rest of this kit — the tokens — is plain
      CSS and works in any stack today.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="binding" title="Binding: attributes, properties, events">
    <p>
      Add <code>CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA</code> to the component that hosts the element, and
      import the element once for its side effect — each one self-registers, so there is
      no module to configure and no <code>customElements.define</code> to call.
    </p>
    {/* tabindex + role + label because the block scrolls horizontally, and a
        scrollable region a keyboard cannot reach is an axe failure the house
        pattern already solves this way. */}
    <pre tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Angular component consuming esa-select"><code>{`import { Component, CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA } from '@angular/core';
import '@esa/ecology/esa-select';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-region-picker',
  standalone: true,
  schemas: [CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA],
  template: \`
    <esa-select label="Region" [options]="regions" (change)="onChange($event)"></esa-select>
  \`,
})
export class RegionPicker {
  regions = [{ value: 'n', label: 'North' }];
  onChange(event: Event) {
    const { value } = (event as CustomEvent).detail;
  }
}`}</code></pre>

    <p>Three rules cover almost everything, and each one fails silently when broken:</p>
    <table class="tk">
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Why it bites</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Read the payload from <code>$event.detail</code></td>
          <td>
            These are <code>CustomEvent</code>s, including the ones named
            <code>change</code>. <code>$event.target.value</code> is empty — no error,
            just nothing.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Write event names exactly as documented</td>
          <td>
            They are lowercase, even when multi-word: <code>pagechange</code>,
            <code>tabchange</code>, <code>featureselect</code>. Angular passes the name
            straight to <code>addEventListener</code>, so <code>(pageChange)</code> binds
            a listener that never fires.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Arrays and objects need <code>[property]</code> binding</td>
          <td>
            <code>[options]</code>, <code>[items]</code>, <code>[data]</code>. Each
            component page's API table names the property and says when it has no
            attribute at all.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      One caution about <code>CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA</code>: it silences unknown-element
      errors <em>and</em> unknown-attribute errors. A typo in an attribute name compiles
      clean and does nothing, so check names against the API table rather than the
      compiler.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="installability" title="What does not work yet">
    <p>
      <strong>You cannot <code>npm install</code> this into a stock Angular CLI build
      today.</strong> The snippets on the component pages are the interop
      <em>contract</em> — element name, attribute versus property, event name and
      <code>detail</code> shape — and they are generated from the live specimen beside
      them, so they cannot drift from what the component really does. They are not yet a
      copy-paste install. Four things are in the way:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>@esa/ecology</code> publishes raw TypeScript from <code>src/</code> with no
        build output and no <code>tsconfig</code>, and the Angular compiler does not
        compile TypeScript inside <code>node_modules</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Its relative imports carry <code>.js</code> extensions — correct ESM, and
        awkward for an unbuilt package.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>astro</code> is a hard peer dependency of the package, even for the Lit
        half that has nothing to do with Astro.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>esa-map</code> imports its stylesheet with Vite's <code>?inline</code>
        suffix, which no other bundler resolves.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of this is Angular-specific and none of it is in the components themselves —
      the elements are standards-only and self-registering. It is a packaging job on the
      hub: a real build step, a loosened peer dependency, and a different way to reach
      that one stylesheet. Until then, treat the Angular tab as the specification you are
      implementing against.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="migrating" title="Migrating an existing Angular app">
    <p>
      Adopting the design system across an app that already exists is a different job from
      using one element, and it has its own tooling: the
      <code>migrate-kit</code> plugin (<code>plugins/migrate-kit/</code>) — an audit, a
      per-component playbook, a per-screen playbook, and a verify stage, driven by the
      app's design spoke.
    </p>
    <p>
      It takes the opposite approach to this page on purpose. Rather than importing the
      Lit elements, it has the app build its own permanent Angular component library
      against the same token and structure contract. That suits a large app with an
      established component layer; importing the elements suits a new surface or a
      gradual adoption. Both target the same tokens, so they converge.
    </p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .tk { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; margin-bottom: var(--spacing-400, 1rem); }
  .tk th { text-align: left; font-weight: 600; font-size: 13px; padding: 10px var(--spacing-400, 1rem); background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken, #f3f7fc); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-default, #dcdcdc); }
  .tk td { padding: 11px var(--spacing-400, 1rem); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-subtle, #efefef); vertical-align: top; color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #525252); }
  .tk tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
  pre { background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken, #f3f7fc); border: 1px solid var(--color-border-default, #dcdcdc); border-radius: 12px; padding: var(--spacing-400, 1rem); overflow-x: auto; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.7; }
  code { font-family: var(--typography-font-family-mono, monospace); font-size: 13px; }
  :not(pre) > code { background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken, #f3f7fc); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 5px; color: var(--color-content-default, #171717); }
  li { margin-bottom: var(--spacing-150, 0.375rem); }
</style>
